WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.414
How the Underwriting Team Evaluates.

2
00:00:02.414 --> 00:00:08.249
Not every deal goes through the same
level of underwriting. A Net-1 card is

3
00:00:08.249 --> 00:00:13.848
evaluated differently than a Net-30 or
Net-60 card — because the risk is

4
00:00:13.848 --> 00:00:19.763
different. On Net-1, the company spends
today and we debit tomorrow. For the

5
00:00:19.763 --> 00:00:25.756
Net1, the primary question is simple: do
they have enough cash in the bank to

6
00:00:25.756 --> 00:00:31.670
cover daily spend? That's why Net-1
doesn't require a balance sheet or P&L —

7
00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:37.821
just a connected bank account, EIN, and
KYC verification. As Tim Jonas puts it:

8
00:00:37.821 --> 00:00:43.420
"If you have cash in the bank, you're
going to get approved for Net-1.".

9
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.752
Extended terms are a different
conversation. On Net-15 or Net-30, we're

10
00:00:48.752 --> 00:00:54.009
carrying days or weeks of credit
exposure. That means the underwriting

11
00:00:54.009 --> 00:01:00.027
team needs to see 24 months of P&L and
balance sheet — in monthly Excel format —

12
00:01:00.027 --> 00:01:05.588
because they're answering a harder
question: can this company reliably pay

13
00:01:05.588 --> 00:01:09.473
us back over time? Here's how that
evaluation works.

14
00:01:09.473 --> 00:01:14.717
The Underwriting team gets on a call with
a prospect. They already read their

15
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:20.236
financials and even if the numbers aren't
great they pick up the phone because as

16
00:01:20.236 --> 00:01:23.962
they put it — "the numbers don't tell the
full story.".

17
00:01:23.962 --> 00:01:29.662
The conversation follows a very specific
pattern — four things they look at every

18
00:01:29.662 --> 00:01:35.007
time: cash runway, current ratio, debt
structure, and revenue trajectory. In

19
00:01:35.007 --> 00:01:40.137
some cases they will ask about the
corporate structure. Learn these five,

20
00:01:40.137 --> 00:01:45.695
and you'll know what the Underwriting
team is going to ask before they asks it.

21
00:01:45.695 --> 00:01:52.479
One: cash runway. This is Underwritings's
North Star. They want to see 12 months of

22
00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:59.097
cash on hand for any prospect requesting
extended terms. This is the bank balance

23
00:01:59.097 --> 00:02:05.220
relative to the credit limit being
requested. A prospect asking for a $500K

24
00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:11.259
daily limit on Net-30 should have
significantly more than $500K sitting in

25
00:02:11.259 --> 00:02:17.547
the bank. If you're on a discovery call
and the prospect says they have three

26
00:02:17.547 --> 00:02:24.082
months of runway, you can rest assured
that a Net-30 won't be feasible. It'll be

27
00:02:24.082 --> 00:02:29.791
a Net-1 application, with a path to
upgrade later on should the runway

28
00:02:29.791 --> 00:02:30.370
extend.

29
00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:36.179
Two: current ratio. This is a company's
current assets divided by current

30
00:02:36.179 --> 00:02:42.715
liabilities. A weak current ratio is the
fastest way to not qualify for Net-30 and

31
00:02:42.715 --> 00:02:48.524
above. If the prospect can't cover their
near-term obligations with their

32
00:02:48.524 --> 00:02:54.333
near-term assets, extending 30 days of
float adds risk they can't absorb.

33
00:02:54.333 --> 00:03:01.043
Three: debt structure. The Underwriting
team always asks about existing financing

34
00:03:01.043 --> 00:03:07.585
— Wayflyer, Settle, Clearco, lines of
credit. Having them isn't a disqualifier.

35
00:03:07.585 --> 00:03:14.127
They want to know if they're being paid
down or re-upped. Feelgoods raised $4.5

36
00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:20.753
million and put 95% of it toward paying
off Wayflyer and Settle, with $3 million

37
00:03:20.753 --> 00:03:27.128
remaining and no plans to re-up those
facilities. The Underwriting team heard

38
00:03:27.128 --> 00:03:33.083
that and it was a green light. Active
debt that's shrinking is a sign of

39
00:03:33.083 --> 00:03:38.870
financial discipline. Debt that keeps
growing is a sign of dependency.

40
00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:43.972
Four: revenue trajectory. We won't just
look at this quarter's revenue.

41
00:03:43.972 --> 00:03:49.511
Underwriting is looking to see if the
prospect trending toward profitability,

42
00:03:49.511 --> 00:03:55.196
or burning cash and working towards a
fundraise? The path towards profitability

43
00:03:55.196 --> 00:04:00.735
comes in different shapes and sizes. A
company losing money on a clear upward

44
00:04:00.735 --> 00:04:04.598
path is different from one losing money
on a flatline.

45
00:04:04.598 --> 00:04:11.098
Five: corporate structure. For prospects
with parent entities, holding companies,

46
00:04:11.098 --> 00:04:17.679
or incubators, The Underwriting team will
explore whether the parent can guarantee

47
00:04:17.679 --> 00:04:23.610
the application. Fridays Health's
standalone financials showed a $10M loss

48
00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:29.622
and negative net asset position — not
approvable on their own. But if their

49
00:04:29.622 --> 00:04:35.797
parent company, Openloop Healthcare,
could provide financials or a guarantee.

50
00:04:35.797 --> 00:04:41.971
Reid, on the underwriting team asked for
exactly that. This is why asking "Is

51
00:04:41.971 --> 00:04:48.065
there a parent company?" in discovery
matters — it can turn a no into a yes.

52
00:04:48.065 --> 00:04:53.623
The takeaway: These five categories are
your pre-flight checklist — cash runway,

53
00:04:53.623 --> 00:04:58.829
current ratio, debt structure, revenue
trajectory, corporate structure. The

54
00:04:58.829 --> 00:05:04.247
better you know them, the more confident
you'll be about the deals you send to

55
00:05:04.247 --> 00:05:09.242
underwriting — and the faster those deals
will move through the process.

56
00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:13.143
Next — what happens when these questions
don't get asked.
